#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""pre-push guard: refuse to publish machine identity, credentials, internal
product names, or CodeCobbler's own survey output to a public remote.

Python 3 stdlib only. No third-party anything, to match the repo it guards.

WHY IT SCANS HISTORY AND NOT THE WORKING TREE
---------------------------------------------
2026-09-17 a history rewrite scrubbed one developer's absolute project path out
of this repo and missed a second spelling of the same path in a Windows batch
file. That spelling stayed reachable from 39 commits and from tags v0.1.0 and
v0.1.1 while the working tree read clean from 2026-09-19 onward.

So a gate that looks at `git diff`, at the index, or at the checkout would have
reported ALL CLEAR every single time while the leak sat in published history.
This gate reads the refs git hands a pre-push hook on stdin and scans the blobs
those refs actually carry to the remote, plus the tip tree, plus commit and tag
messages.

WHY THE THINGS IT BLOCKS ARE NOT WRITTEN DOWN HERE
--------------------------------------------------
2026-09-20: this file is committed to two PUBLIC repositories. Until today its
rule table listed, in plaintext, every private product name and every machine
literal it exists to keep out -- so the guard published the exact inventory it
was built to suppress, and `grep -i` over a public clone read it straight off.

Those two rule tables are now SHA-256 digests of the lowercased names (see
`_token_finder`). Scanning hashes each candidate identifier token out of the
text and looks the digest up, instead of matching a pattern that spells the
name. This is NOT secrecy: a short known word is dictionary-attackable in
seconds, and it is meant to be, because the guard has to be able to recognise
the name with nothing but what is in this file. What it removes is the
BROWSABLE INVENTORY -- the part that actually did the damage. An attacker who
already knows a name can confirm it; an attacker who does not cannot read it.

Consequences, stated so nobody quietly undoes them:
  * the guard cannot print a hashed match in plaintext, and does not try; a
    hashed finding reports rule, file and line and withholds the text, so the
    name does not land in a terminal, a CI log or the override log.
  * this file is no longer exempt from content scanning. It has no private
    string left in it, so it can be scanned like anything else -- and if
    someone ever pastes one back in, the guard blocks its own push. That is a
    stronger regression test than any unit test.
  * to add a name, run `pre-push --hash NAME` and paste the digest. Do not
    write the name into this file, not even in a comment.

CONTRACT (githooks(5), verified against git's own pre-push.sample)
------------------------------------------------------------------
  argv[1] = remote name, argv[2] = remote URL (equal when pushing a bare URL)
  stdin   = one line per ref:  <local ref> SP <local oid> SP <remote ref> SP <remote oid> LF
  local ref is the literal "(delete)" and local oid is all-zeroes for a deletion
  remote oid is all-zeroes when the remote ref does not exist yet
  non-zero exit aborts the whole push, nothing is sent
"""

